1953125, 0.017181396484375, 0.03070068359375, -0.0161285400390625, -0.0014171600341796875, -0.006343841552734375, 0.021392822265625, -0.0168914794921875, -0.01317596435546875, 0.046844482421875, -0.0228424072265625, 0.020263671875, 0.016845703125, 0.005725860595703125, 0.040008544921875, 0.020965576171875, 0.035980224609375, -0.01183319091796875, 0.0367431640625, 0.00737762451171875, 0.01934814453125, -0.019073486328125, 0.005290985107421875, 0.01195526123046875, -0.007511138916015625, 0.00572967529296875, -0.01885986328125, 0.003841400146484375, -0.0094451904296875, 0.055145263671875, -0.00911712646484375, -0.01441192626953125, -0.0235137939453125, -0.0017242431640625, -0.005523681640625, -0.013092041015625, 0.0194549560546875, 0.0226898193359375, 0.0036106109619140625, -0.0180511474609375, -0.0116119384765625, -0.0185089111328125, 0.00841522216796875, -0.02752685546875, 0.027679443359375, -0.044952392578125, -0.00698089599609375, -0.01056671142578125, -0.0014333724975585938, 0.0157623291015625, -0.0013246536254882812, -0.005573272705078125, -0.005695343017578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines optimize datasets for individual tasks in isolation, limiting the ability to leverage shared knowledge across related generative tasks. Our work introduces a fundamentally new approach: a capability-driven data infrastructure that systematically organizes and integrates supervision for multiple generative capabilities, enabling curriculum-based training and interoperability across tasks. Unlike prior work, which typically focuses on single-task curation or ad hoc multi-task extensions, our framework explicitly models task dependencies, integrates data engines with shared annotation layers, and schedules data exposure according to a principled curriculum.\n\nThe capability-driven data infrastructure consists of three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct supervision type, and a curriculum scheduler that sequences data presentation. Concretely, the infrastructure operates as follows:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:** This engine constructs datasets by crawling public web sources and open repositories for image-caption pairs. We use CLIP ViT-L/14 (OpenAI, 2021) for relevance scoring, retaining pairs with a cosine similarity above 0.28 (empirically determined for a 95% precision rate on a validation set). Language model toxicity checks are performed using OpenAI GPT-3.5-turbo with a toxicity threshold of 0.2 (Perspective API scale). A random 5% sample is selected for human review, stratified by source and content type, with annotators trained using a 100-example calibration set and required to achieve Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8 before contributing. Caption experts are implemented as BLIP-2 models (Li et al., 2023) fine-tuned on 1M human-annotated captions; these standardize and enrich captions using a fixed prompt template. The engine outputs a corpus of 440M high-quality image-caption pairs.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:** This engine assembles editing pairs by generating synthetic edits (using Adobe Photoshop batch scripts for color/style changes and Segment Anything Model for object edits) and collecting real-world edits from the HIVE and MIT-Adobe FiveK datasets. Each pair includes the original image, the edited image, and a standardized transformation instruction generated by the same BLIP-2 caption experts. Consistency checks require a minimum 0.85 cosine similarity between the transformation instruction embedding and the actual edit description (using Sentence-BERT). Annotators validate a stratified 5% sample, with inter-annotator agreement >0.78. The engine produces 120M editing pairs.\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:** This engine links images to structured knowledge entities by applying entity recognition with BLIP-2 and mapping to Wikidata using entity linking heuristics (minimum confidence 0.7). Manual validation is performed on a stratified 10% sample, with annotators trained on a 50-example gold standard and agreement measured (Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8). The engine outputs 27M image-entity pairs.\n\n**Interoperability and Data Integration:** Outputs from the three engines are merged via a shared annotation schema (JSON-LD). For example, an image-caption pair is linked to its knowledge entities and can serve as a base for generating editing pairs. Conflicts (e.g., inconsistent captions or entity mappings) are resolved by majority voting among three independent caption experts and two human annotators, with ties broken by a senior annotator. Redundancies are detected by hashing CLIP image embeddings and deduplicating at the dataset level.\n\n**Curriculum Learning Strategy:** The curriculum scheduler organizes data presentation in three stages: (1) T2I grounding, (2) editing, and (3) knowledge association. Stage transitions are triggered when validation loss (cross-entropy for T2I, L2 for editing, entity accuracy for knowledge) plateaus for 3 consecutive epochs (<0.1% change) and task-specific thresholds are met (e.g., FID < 10 for T2I, edit accuracy >85% before introducing knowledge data). These thresholds are set based on ablation studies: models trained with T2I first, then editing, then knowledge, outperform all other orderings by 3\u20135% FID and 4% editing accuracy (see Table 1). The rationale is that foundational grounding skills facilitate more complex transformations and associations.\n\n**Evaluation Methodology:** We train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at 3B and 6B parameters using the curated datasets. Training is conducted on 512 A100 GPUs for 14 days (3B) and 28 days (6B), with mixed-precision optimization and fixed random seeds. Dataset splits are 90% train, 5% validation, 5% test, stratified by source, content, and demographic attributes. Baselines include Stable Diffusion v2.1 (HuggingFace config), Imagen Editor (official release), and a BLIP-2-based T2I model, each fine-tuned on our training split for 5 epochs with identical augmentation and optimizer settings. CPI-Bench comprises 50K prompts balanced across domains (objects, scenes, edits, knowledge), with 10K held out for testing. Metrics include FID, CLIP score, and editing accuracy (measured by pixel-level IoU for edits); statistical significance is assessed via paired t-tests (\u03b1=0.01, power >0.95). Human preference scores are collected from 50 expert raters (minimum 2 years experience), each evaluating 200 randomly sampled generations per model, with inter-rater agreement measured by Fleiss\u2019 kappa (>0.75).\n\n**Summary of Figures 2\u20134:** Figure 2 diagrams the data engine workflows, showing the flow from raw data to filtered, annotated, and integrated datasets, with sample annotation schemas. Figure 3 presents qualitative examples: (a) T2I generations for complex prompts (e.g., \"a red fox in a snowy forest at dusk\"), (b) editing pairs (original, instruction, edited output), and (c) image-entity associations (image, caption, linked Wikidata entity). Examples are randomly sampled from the test set. Figure 4 quantitatively compares our models to baselines: our 6B model achieves FID 8.2 (vs. 9.1 for Stable Diffusion), CLIP score 0.41 (vs. 0.38), and edit accuracy 89% (vs. 84%). Human raters prefer our outputs 62% of the time (p < 0.01).\n\n**Bias Mitigation and Quality Control:** Automated filters remove images with detected sensitive content (using NSFW classifiers from LAION-5B) and flag demographic imbalances (using FairFace attribute classifiers for gender, age, and ethnicity). Demographic groups are defined per FairFace: 7 ethnicities, 2 genders, 4 age brackets. Human annotators review all flagged samples; annotators are trained on a 200-example bias calibration set and must achieve >0.85 agreement. Dataset composition is adjusted to ensure demographic parity (parity score = min(group proportion)/max(group proportion), target >0.9). Effectiveness is validated by comparing model outputs for bias using the FairFace benchmark, with observed bias (mean difference in output rates across groups) reduced by 30% (p < 0.01) relative to baselines.\n\n**Computational Resources and Reproducibility:** All experiments are conducted on a cluster of 512 A100 GPUs, with training times, hyperparameters, and random seeds logged in a public experiment tracker. Code (Apache 2.0), data processing scripts, and model checkpoints (subject to third-party data licensing) will be released within 3 months of publication. Data containing personal information is excluded or anonymized per GDPR guidelines. An ethics statement and data usage policy will accompany the release.\n\nOur models achieve competitive or superior performance on CPI-Bench: the 6B model achieves FID 8.2 (vs. 9.1 for Stable Diffusion), CLIP score 0.41 (vs. 0.38), and edit accuracy 89% (vs. 84%). Human raters prefer our outputs 62% of the time (p < 0.01). Qualitative results (Figure 3) demonstrate broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer between generative tasks, with ablation studies confirming the benefit of our curriculum and data integration approach."}}